since writing my response to Ari's torch idea i've amended my suggestions
which will follow. let me raise these questions and make these points first:
* what is the effect of quaffing a potion of oil?
* stones (with the inclusion of simple whetstones) have their own set of
  attributes. flintstones could add to that but do they have to?
* what are the compelling advantages to having a 5x5 (or other) lighted
  area? there's no inherent problem with the lighting code as it exists.
* the torch ideas given could be integrated with existing code very easily
  along the lines i suggested because the lighting code already exists,
  "scare monster" code already exists, fire attack code already exists
  and so on. given that the "templates" are already there the
  implementation would, to me, seem obvious ...
* the gist of my suggestions was to make the torch a weapon (that perhaps
  a priest would start with). if a torch comes into contact with flame it
  ignites, becoming a "burning torch", which is a better weapon that has
  other possiblities (which to some extent already exist).

the amended concept:
a torch that comes into contact with fire would ignite becoming a burning
torch with a lengthy duration between 1000 and 2000 moves. another weapon
has been observed to change under the appropriate treatment. picking up or
merely unwielding a burning torch doesn't put it out. however dropping it
(whereupon it has a scare monster effect) and engraving with it should
shorten its term. a spellbook of fireball would also become more valuable
in this scenario since you could zap the spell on yourself and ignite
the torch. perhaps the priest should always start with a spellbook of
fire (and damnation), be fire resistant, and carry a torch.

any room you enter wielding a burning torch would automatically be lit.
wielding a burning torch would light up a dark room. additionally the
torch can be applied, directionally, doing fire damage to adjacent
objects.

if you are really unlucky you would notice that an unwielded burning
torch in your inventory would occasionally do random fire damage
otherwise it is not a problem (with dexterity factored in). some of
the possible ways to ignite a torch would be:
1) the tower of flame from a magic trap
2) the tower of flame from a scroll of fire
3) the fire breath weapon of dragons, nagas or hellhounds hitting you
4) spell of fire cast upon yourself from a wand or spellbook
5) a 10% chance that kicking a torch would ignite it and a 5% chance
   that kicking a torch would destroy it completely (i know this is
   far-fetched ...). if a kicked torch ignited and went flying across
   the room you might not notice unless it landed on some leather goods
   or burnable objects in which case you'd smell smoke.
other means are possible and have been suggested to me ...

a burning torch ought to be cherished as a precious rare item.

this is not an "us against them" or "right or wrong" situation.
there's plenty of room for anybody's ideas here ...

